WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.010
Don't know your Weapon X from your X-Men?

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:04.480
No idea what a "snikt" is?

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.467
Fear not — here's everything you need to
know about Hugh Jackman's Wolverine.

00:00:08.797 --> 00:00:14.110
While X-Men might have been a popular franchise
for Marvel Comics at the tail end of the 1990s,

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:16.680
comic book movies weren't exactly in vogue.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:22.590
After the disastrous response to 1997's Batman
&amp; Robin shook Hollywood to its core, the superhero

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.300
genre became Kryptonite for Hollywood actors.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.750
Despite this sense of foreboding, many stars
were linked to the part of Wolverine at this

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:33.840
time, with some openly throwing their hat
into the X-Men casting ring.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.460
Before Hugh Jackman grew out his mutton chops
and learned to say "bub," Scottish actor Dougray

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.610
Scott was cast as Wolverine in Bryan Singer's
first X-movie.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:46.430
Unfortunately, his work on Mission: Impossible
II ran into overtime, and he was forced to

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:50.430
drop out of X-Men by, of all people, Tom Cruise.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.210
Scott told The Telegraph in 2020:

00:01:04.811 --> 00:01:08.982
Instead, Hugh Jackman found his way into the
movie — and the hearts of millions.

00:01:09.123 --> 00:01:13.710
The casting of Hugh Jackman as Wolverine caught
many people in the movie industry off-guard

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.666
— including the actor himself.

00:01:15.666 --> 00:01:20.000
In a later interview with Jimmy Fallon, Jackman
revealed that his Wolverine audition was only

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.090
20 seconds long.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.290
At the time, he saw it as nothing more than
a box-ticking exercise on the studio's part,

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.820
since Dougray Scott had already been cast
in the role.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:32.885
"When I walked into that room, I was pretty
sure that I wasn't playing the role."

00:01:32.885 --> 00:01:36.530
But it seemed that 20th Century Fox wanted
to hedge their bets.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:40.571
"Dougray Scott had the role, and then he got
caught up on Mission: Impossible II, but everyone

00:01:40.571 --> 00:01:41.799
thought that would get sorted.

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:45.460
But the studio said, 'Look, we need a backup
plan just in case.'"

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.820
Jackman explained that this took the pressure
off him during his audition, since he didn't

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.030
believe there was any chance he'd get the
part.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.460
He just went with the flow.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:56.369
Kevin Feige, who was an associate producer
on the first "X-Men" film, even offered to

00:01:56.369 --> 00:01:58.579
drive him to the airport and get a bite to
eat.

00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:02.189
The actor told him that he didn't have to
go out of his way to be nice, however, as

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:05.680
he knew the chances of him getting the part
were extremely slim.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.608
And we all know what happened next.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.530
Hugh Jackman has always worn his heart on
his sleeve when it comes to his wife, Deborra -Lee Furness.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.200
In an Instagram post celebrating their 25th
anniversary, Jackman wrote:

00:02:21.494 --> 00:02:24.170
But Jackman and Furness aren't always in perfect
agreement.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:28.270
In fact, if it was up to his wife, Jackman
would have passed on the role of Wolverine

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:29.270
in X-Men.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:30.630
The actor later told Vulture:

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.879
Jackman explained that they were both confused
by some of the comic book terminology used,

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:45.879
such as the "snikt!"

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:49.152
sound that Wolverine's claws make when they
pop out of his knuckles.

00:02:49.152 --> 00:02:52.332
But Jackman took the leap, regardless of his
wife's apprehension.

00:02:52.883 --> 00:02:57.140
In an interview with Female Magazine, Hugh
Jackman admitted he knew nothing about the

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:00.260
X-Men comic book series before he received
the movie's script.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.230
However, he liked what he read on the page,
and could easily tell Logan was a resonant

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.459
character with enormous potential.

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:10.360
But even though Jackman was excited about
the gig, the people around him tried to temper

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.360
his expectations.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:12.400
He explained:

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:31.010
Though this advice might sound strange today,
these were the days before the superhero boom

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.189
changed cinema as we know it.

00:03:33.189 --> 00:03:36.739
Back then, making a comic book movie was basically
a coin flip.

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:40.970
Maybe the people adapting the comics in question
had vision, passion for the source material,

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.820
and the talent to pull it off — or maybe
they were simply trying to cash in on a recognizable brand.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.780
Fortunately, the word on the street was completely
off the mark in this instance.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:54.319
In fact, there's an argument to be made that
the box office success of X-Men is what led

00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:57.200
to Hollywood's renewed interest in superhero
movies.

00:03:57.638 --> 00:04:00.400
"You don't have to do this.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.234
All right, maybe you do."

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:07.845
Out of the three Wolverine films, the one
that gets the worst rap is X-Men Origins: Wolverine.

00:04:07.845 --> 00:04:12.330
Directed by Gavin Hood, Origins earnestly
attempts to bring Wolverine's complex beginnings

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:13.164
to the screen.

00:04:13.242 --> 00:04:17.699
But many of its artistic choices, such as
the treatment of Deadpool and Gambit's tragic

00:04:17.699 --> 00:04:20.989
lack of screen time, left fans and critics
disappointed.

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:25.100
The movie might have been doomed from the
start, however, no matter what creative decisions

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:29.300
it made, as an unfinished copy was leaked
onto the internet more than a month before

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.080
it was released in U.S. theaters.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.460
Speaking to the media at the time, Jackman
remarked:

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:43.850
The actor added that pirates who downloaded
the movie were watching an incomplete creation,

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:47.150
which simply couldn't be judged according
to typical metrics.

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:51.340
Though this rough version had already been
watched by a vast number of people before

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:56.750
the film arrived in cinemas, X-Men Origins:
Wolverine still managed to make a decent $373

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:59.250
million off a $150 million budget.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:04.130
In 2011, the mystery of who leaked the movie
was solved when a New Yorker named Gilberto

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:07.910
Sanchez pleaded guilty to uploading the unfinished
film onto the internet.

00:05:08.344 --> 00:05:11.050
There are no sacred cows in Deadpool.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:15.410
The first film in the series takes shots at
pretty much anything it can, from its own

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:19.289
lack of budget to Wolverine and even Hugh
Jackman himself.

00:05:19.601 --> 00:05:20.191
"Ha!

00:05:20.289 --> 00:05:22.630
Fake laugh, hiding real pain."

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:27.470
The franchise doesn't stop there, either — if
anything, Deadpool 2 amps up the insults.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:31.160
While it appears to be all in good fun, some
people might not take too kindly to being

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:33.160
the butt of an oft-repeated joke.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.848
But Hugh Jackman actually loved these quips,
as Deadpool co-writer Rhett Reese told Yahoo!,

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:51.660
Reese added that they brought a number of
questions to the actor directly, especially

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.150
when it came to jokes that used his likeness.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:58.380
Fortunately, Jackman found everything they
suggested hilarious, and encouraged them to

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:00.114
have some fun at his expense.

00:06:00.639 --> 00:06:04.880
Every studio would love to sign an actor up
to play a superhero forever, especially if

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:06.229
the money is rolling in.

00:06:06.229 --> 00:06:10.590
The truth is, though, actors usually want
to portray a wide variety of different characters

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.300
over the course of their careers.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.199
Committing to a singular role for a long stretch
of time is a very rare occurrence.

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:20.840
Normally, a contract may be for a few years,
or a couple of movies, but even then, stars

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.160
seem to count down the days before they can
hang up their spandex.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.180
Hugh Jackman is a notable exception to this
rule.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.680
In his first stint, he played Wolverine for
nearly 17 years, eventually going out on his

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.280
own terms in Logan.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.090
No one could fault him for that, after almost
two decades at the wheel.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:40.560
It's a remarkable achievement, and a record-breaking
one at that.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.560
In 2019, both Jackman and Patrick Stewart
received Guinness World Records certificates

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.639
for having the longest careers as live-action
Marvel superheroes.

00:06:48.639 --> 00:06:53.272
And that was before both Stewart and Jackman
returned to their respective roles.

00:06:53.589 --> 00:06:58.460
When Hugh Jackman retired as Wolverine for
the first time, he did so on a high.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:03.910
Logan is not only one of the best X-Men films,
but arguably one of the best superhero movies

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:05.069
of all time.

00:07:05.069 --> 00:07:09.410
It's not surprising, then, that nobody was
really clamoring for Jackman to go.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:14.660
Even before Logan blew fans and critics away,
Jackman's Wolverine was the most consistently

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:20.840
high-quality aspect of a franchise that fluctuated
wildly between brilliance and trash.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.680
But Jackman wanted to go out on top, rather
than limp to the finish line.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:28.460
In an interview with Jimmy Fallon, Jackman
explained that he once met Jerry Seinfeld

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:32.630
and asked the comedian how he knew when it
was time to pull the plug on his long-running

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:33.630
show, Seinfeld.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:36.995
"When you're creating something it's very
important not to run yourself dry.

00:07:36.995 --> 00:07:40.698
It's not about finishing on top, necessarily,
but making sure that you're, creatively, still

00:07:40.698 --> 00:07:42.069
got something left."

00:07:42.069 --> 00:07:46.250
The actor added that he immediately went home
and told his wife that he was hanging up the

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:47.250
claws for good.

00:07:47.918 --> 00:07:52.500
In the world of comic book movies, there's
just one certainty: It's only over if the

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:53.870
money says it is.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:59.400
Yet James Mangold's Logan positions itself
as the final chapter in a legacy, one from

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.250
which there is no going back.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:06.160
And once the movie was released, it was made
abundantly clear that this version of Logan

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:07.240
was no more.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:11.710
At a special screening of Logan Noir, Mangold
and Hugh Jackman revealed more about how they

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:13.020
approached the film.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:17.210
For one thing, their initial discussions didn't
actually cover whether Logan would survive

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:21.210
or not — but they felt they needed something
definitive to mark the end of the franchise

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:22.583
and the character's story.

00:08:22.583 --> 00:08:23.583
Mangold said:

00:08:32.605 --> 00:08:36.640
When fans learned that the X-Men would be
joining the Marvel Cinematic Universe, the

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:41.409
rumor mill went into overdrive regarding who
would succeed Hugh Jackman as Wolverine.

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:45.660
Some fans thought Daniel Radcliffe would be
a good pick, while others swore only Tom Hardy

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:47.520
should be in consideration for the role.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.155
There was even a passionate petition for Danny
DeVito to play the hero.

00:08:51.155 --> 00:08:53.115
"Oh, you unzipped me!

00:08:53.193 --> 00:08:54.724
It's all coming back!

00:08:55.485 --> 00:08:57.306
It's all coming back, I hate you!"

00:08:57.462 --> 00:09:02.339
Everyone has their favorites and is eager
to make their case — even Jackman himself.

00:09:02.339 --> 00:09:05.920
Before announcing his return for Deadpool
3, Jackman weighed in with his own choice

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:07.850
of actor for the iconic role.

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:11.920
Speaking to film critic Rajeev Masand, Jackman
suggested Indian actor Shah Rukh Khan should

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:13.150
be a contender.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:18.030
Jackman admires the Bollywood performer enormously,
and, as it turns out, Khan is a big fan of

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:20.120
both Wolverine and Jackman.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:25.130
He praised Jackman effusively at an Indian
film convention in 2011, and said his fellow

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:29.021
actor had once outright offered him the opportunity
to be part of an X-Men film.

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:35.090
James Mangold's Logan serves as the perfect
send-off for Hugh Jackman's Wolverine: It's

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:38.620
raw, emotional, and surprisingly beautiful.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:42.640
Fans flocked to the theater to say their fond
farewell to one of the most iconic cinematic

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.279
interpretations of a classic superhero, while
Jackman himself stated this was the end of

00:09:47.279 --> 00:09:49.490
his journey as the Ol' Canucklehead.

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:54.870
So, imagine the surprise of comic book fans
everywhere when Ryan Reynolds revealed Jackman

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.870
would be returning as Wolverine for Deadpool
3.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.210
Once the celebration and chatter settled,
many wondered what Mangold thought of this,

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:07.220
since the director made Logan with every intention
of it being the end for Jackman's Wolverine.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:11.540
They didn't have to wait long to find out:
Mangold posted a GIF of Logan's demise on

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.940
Twitter without comment, leading the internet
to draw its own conclusion.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:19.532
But he soon posted another Tweet clarifying
there were no hard feelings at all — and

00:10:19.532 --> 00:10:23.576
that he couldn't wait to see what his friends
would get up to in Deadpool 3.

